WEBVTT

legitimacy-01-1
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:05.430
Have you ever looked closely at the first
page of your passport?

legitimacy-01-2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:11.320
On that page stand the three pillars of
the modern state.

legitimacy-01-3
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:17.460
Episode 1: Three Terms, One Closed Loop

legitimacy-01-4
00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:24.010
Open it. This is a fictional passport,
but the format will look familiar.

legitimacy-01-5
00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:34.731
Pause here: find three things on these
two pages—a name, an authority, and a

legitimacy-01-6
00:00:34.731 --> 00:00:35.860
request.

legitimacy-01-7
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:42.042
The name “Country X”; the authority that
issued it; and a request addressed to

legitimacy-01-8
00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:43.310
other countries.

legitimacy-01-9
00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:48.090
First, look at “Nationality: Country X.”

legitimacy-01-10
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:52.311
The national anthem plays before a match.
She knows none of the tens of thousands

legitimacy-01-11
00:00:52.311 --> 00:00:53.040
in the stands.

legitimacy-01-12
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.150
Yet she feels they are all part of “us.”

legitimacy-01-13
00:00:56.150 --> 00:01:00.830
Nation: it gives you a “we,” while
drawing a line around those outside.

legitimacy-01-14
00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:04.831
Anderson, 1983: a nation is an “imagined
community.” Most members never meet, yet

legitimacy-01-15
00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:06.560
share a sense of “we.” (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-01-16
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.900
Next, look at “Issuing authority.”

legitimacy-01-17
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.027
“We” is a feeling. Who you are, and
whether you can enter, are settled

legitimacy-01-18
00:01:16.027 --> 00:01:18.680
through counters, stamps, gates, and
borders.

legitimacy-01-19
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.070
State: it turns “we” into rules,
documents, and procedures.

legitimacy-01-20
00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:25.547
Weber, 1919 (Gerth &amp; Mills's 1946 English
translation): a state is a human

legitimacy-01-21
00:01:25.547 --> 00:01:28.090
community that successfully monopolizes
the legitimate use of force within a

legitimacy-01-22
00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:28.860
territory. (Paraphrase)

legitimacy-01-23
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.420
Finally, the request addressed to other
countries.

legitimacy-01-24
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.840
Sovereignty: having the final say within
this territory.

legitimacy-01-25
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.242
Normally, everyone follows the rules.
When the rules fail, who can say, “The

legitimacy-01-26
00:01:43.242 --> 00:01:44.690
rules are now suspended”?

legitimacy-01-27
00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:47.365
Schmitt: “Sovereign is he who decides on
the exception.” (Political Theology,

legitimacy-01-28
00:01:47.365 --> 00:01:48.790
Schwab's English translation, 2005, p. 5)

legitimacy-01-29
00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.854
Having the final say at home is not
enough. The request addresses other

legitimacy-01-30
00:01:52.854 --> 00:01:56.690
countries: please recognize this
passport, and this state, as real.

legitimacy-01-31
00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:58.866
Real example: the first page of a British
passport asks, in the King's name, that

legitimacy-01-32
00:01:58.866 --> 00:02:00.854
“all those whom it may concern” let the
bearer pass and provide assistance

legitimacy-01-33
00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.896
(royal.uk). “Sovereignty is also a
currency of international recognition” is

legitimacy-01-34
00:02:02.896 --> 00:02:03.890
the author's argument in thesis §2.2.

legitimacy-01-35
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:11.550
We are a nation—so we should have our own
state.

legitimacy-01-36
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:15.460
We have a state—so we can claim
sovereignty.

legitimacy-01-37
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:20.237
We have sovereignty and recognition from
other states—so we really are a

legitimacy-01-38
00:02:20.237 --> 00:02:21.100
nation-state.

legitimacy-01-39
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:26.767
Each pillar is vouched for by the other
two. The author calls this a recursive

legitimacy-01-40
00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:27.930
legitimacy loop.

legitimacy-01-41
00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:34.030
Choose one: which came first, the nation
or the state?

legitimacy-01-42
00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:40.642
Inside this loop, the question has no
starting point. It circles every day,

legitimacy-01-43
00:02:40.642 --> 00:02:43.110
making it seem self-evident.

legitimacy-01-44
00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.750
But some people do not have this page.

legitimacy-01-45
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:54.740
No state recognizes him as its national.
Such a person is stateless.

legitimacy-01-46
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:59.720
The loop creates not only an “us,” but
also people it keeps outside.

legitimacy-01-47
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:06.390
In two episodes, we will explore why
there is no “us” without “them.”

legitimacy-01-48
00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:10.330
This episode's reference card (take a
screenshot)

legitimacy-01-49
00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:14.120
Nation | “Nationality: Country X” | Gives
identity: a “we.”

legitimacy-01-50
00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.390
State | The issuing authority's stamp |
Provides governance: rules and documents.

legitimacy-01-51
00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:24.538
Sovereignty | The request to other
countries | Provides final authority and

legitimacy-01-52
00:03:24.538 --> 00:03:26.260
external recognition.

legitimacy-01-53
00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.820
The three pillars vouch for one another,
making them stable.

legitimacy-01-54
00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:36.330
But what if, one day, people stop
believing in one of them?

legitimacy-01-55
00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:43.154
This series is called Legitimacy Dies
Twice. The first death is the nation's

legitimacy-01-56
00:03:43.154 --> 00:03:48.810
symbolic order; the second is what
dissenters put in its place.

legitimacy-01-57
00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.470
Next episode:

legitimacy-01-58
00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:58.650
How is a “we” made on a production line?

legitimacy-01-59
00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:05.850
This is UjiBricks.
